## Proof

Let $\theta_k = \dfrac{k\pi}{2n}$, so $\theta_k - \theta_{k-1} = \dfrac{\pi}{2n}$ and $\theta_{k-1}+\theta_k = \dfrac{(2k-1)\pi}{2n}$. The summand is

$$T_k = \frac{\sin(\theta_{k-1}+\theta_k)}{\cos^2\theta_{k-1}\,\cos^2\theta_k}.$$

**Key identities.** Using $\tan\alpha \pm \tan\beta = \dfrac{\sin(\alpha\pm\beta)}{\cos\alpha\cos\beta}$:

$$\tan\theta_k + \tan\theta_{k-1} = \frac{\sin(\theta_k+\theta_{k-1})}{\cos\theta_k\cos\theta_{k-1}},\qquad \tan\theta_k - \tan\theta_{k-1} = \frac{\sin(\theta_k-\theta_{k-1})}{\cos\theta_k\cos\theta_{k-1}} = \frac{\sin(\pi/(2n))}{\cos\theta_k\cos\theta_{k-1}}.$$

Multiplying these two equations:

$$(\tan\theta_k+\tan\theta_{k-1})(\tan\theta_k-\tan\theta_{k-1}) = \frac{\sin(\theta_k+\theta_{k-1})\,\sin(\pi/(2n))}{\cos^2\theta_k\cos^2\theta_{k-1}},$$

i.e.

$$T_k = \frac{\tan^2\theta_k - \tan^2\theta_{k-1}}{\sin(\pi/(2n))}.$$

**Telescoping.** Summing $k=1$ to $n-1$:

$$a_n = \frac{1}{\sin(\pi/(2n))}\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\bigl(\tan^2\theta_k - \tan^2\theta_{k-1}\bigr) = \frac{\tan^2\theta_{n-1} - \tan^2\theta_0}{\sin(\pi/(2n))}.$$

Since $\theta_0 = 0$ and $\theta_{n-1} = \dfrac{(n-1)\pi}{2n} = \dfrac{\pi}{2} - \dfrac{\pi}{2n}$, we have $\tan\theta_0 = 0$ and

$$\tan\theta_{n-1} = \tan\!\left(\tfrac{\pi}{2}-\tfrac{\pi}{2n}\right) = \cot\!\left(\tfrac{\pi}{2n}\right) = \frac{\cos(\pi/(2n))}{\sin(\pi/(2n))}.$$

Therefore

$$a_n = \frac{\cos^2(\pi/(2n))}{\sin^3(\pi/(2n))}.$$

**The limit.** Using $\sin x \sim x$ as $x\to 0$ with $x = \pi/(2n)$:

$$n\,\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2n}\right) \to \frac{\pi}{2},\qquad \cos^2\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2n}\right)\to 1.$$

Hence

$$\frac{a_n}{n^3} = \frac{\cos^2(\pi/(2n))}{\bigl(n\,\sin(\pi/(2n))\bigr)^3} \;\longrightarrow\; \frac{1}{(\pi/2)^3} = \frac{8}{\pi^3}.$$

$$\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{a_n}{n^3} = \frac{8}{\pi^3}}$$

